---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Source from '../components/Source.astro';
import { DEMOS, REPO } from '../lib/figures';

const limacon = DEMOS.find((d) => d.id === 'limacon')!;
const circle = DEMOS.find((d) => d.id === 'circle')!;
const [plainCircle, bandedCircle] = circle.looks;
---
<Base figures>
  <div class="page">
    <section class="hero">
      <div>
        <h1>Pencil</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A declarative language for mathematical figures. A figure is a text file that says what’s
          in it and what each thing means, and style sheets say how it looks. The same file renders
          as an interactive figure on the web and as video.
        </p>
        <p class="status">
          <strong>It’s early, and the syntax will change.</strong> Pencil is being built to make math
          explainers, one real figure at a time, and nothing about the language is stable yet.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="primary" href="/docs/getting-started/">Get started</a>
          <a href="/demos/">See the demos</a>
          <a href="/playground/">Open the playground</a>
          <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="plate" style="margin: 0">
        <Figure id="hero" looks={limacon.looks} label={limacon.label} controls={false} priority />
        <figcaption>
          Twenty-four circles through one point, each warm inside and cool outside, adding up. Their
          envelope is a limaçon. <a href="/demos/#limacon">Its document</a> is nine lines, and its sheet four rules.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="example">
      <h2 id="example">One figure, two style sheets</h2>
      <div class="example-doc">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            A document names things and says what they are. This one says there’s a curve <code>c</code>, the points at distance <var>r</var> from the origin, and that <var>r</var> breathes with time. It holds no colors and no sizes.
          </p>
          <p>
            A sheet targets the name <code>c</code>. Because the curve is implicit, it has a signed distance, so a sheet can style its inside and outside. The second sheet bands both sides by that distance and moves the hue with time, and not a line of the document changes.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Source path={plainCircle.pen} />
      </div>
      <div class="example">
        <div class="look">
          <Figure id="circle-plain" looks={[plainCircle]} label={circle.label} controls={false} />
          <Source path={plainCircle.sheets[0]} />
        </div>
        <div class="look">
          <Figure id="circle-bands" looks={[bandedCircle]} label={`${circle.label} Its sides are banded by their distance from it.`} controls={false} />
          <Source path={bandedCircle.sheets[0]} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="whats-in-it">
      <h2 id="whats-in-it">What’s in it</h2>
      <ul class="features">
        <li>
          <strong>Expressions, not drawing calls.</strong>
          Points are complex numbers, derivatives are exact, and a <code>solve</code> can find
          unknowns each frame. A figure compiles to an IR with its expressions in MathJSON, so it
          isn’t locked into this tool.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Time and input are the same thing.</strong>
          Animation and interaction both bind the params of one figure, so what a reader drags and
          what a video plays are one description.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Collections and maps.</strong>
          One declaration can draw thousands of dots, circles or curves, and can go through a
          function, so one thing can appear in several panels.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Strokes and sides that follow the math.</strong>
          A circle has a handedness and can pass through a line, and both of its sides can be drawn
          at once. A taper can follow arc length.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Description, style and rendering stay apart.</strong>
          A figure holds no colors, sizes or renderer-specific calls. Styles target its names, with
          CSS-like rules and math-specific properties.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Two renderers that check each other.</strong>
          A Rust core compiles figures to WGSL for WebGPU and WebGL2, and a separate CPU interpreter
          defines what each pixel should be. <code>pencil check</code> compares them.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="where">
      <h2 id="where">Where to go</h2>
      <ul class="paths">
        <li>
          <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Getting started</a>
          <p>Build it, render a frame, compare the GPU with the CPU, and export video.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/docs/language/">Language reference</a>
          <p>Statements, curve forms, expressions, built-ins and styles.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/demos/">Demos</a>
          <p>Every figure in the corpus, drawn live, with its source.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/playground/">Playground</a>
          <p>Edit any figure and its sheets in the browser, and watch it recompile.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={REPO}>Source</a>
          <p>The Rust core, the renderer, the command and the browser host, under MIT.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="status" class="prose">
      <h2 id="status">Where it stands</h2>
      <p>
        Pencil is built from a corpus of real figures, and a feature goes in only when a figure needs
        it. The syntax and the IR change freely while that goes on, so expect files written today to
        need edits later.
      </p>
      <p>
        Figures here are drawn on your GPU, through WebGPU where the browser has it and WebGL2
        otherwise. They’ve been checked mostly in Chrome on a Mac; other browsers and phones much
        less. If a figure doesn’t draw, the page says why under it.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
